From 84cc8e879529bd319c68a68f5b1976a9067512ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:22:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(entities): evaluate named actor properties and
 pre-animation

Retain immutable named actor property snapshots across sparse updates and
bind query.property and query.has_property before pre_animation and render
controller selection. Reevaluate custom actor selections on ticks so a
property or time-driven controller can change the visible model.

Correct Mocha parser and evaluator boundaries for string equality,
assignment/ternary precedence and unary calls. CubeCraft controllers use
all three behaviors, and Geyser mushroom permutations negate property
queries. Shared semantic tests cover enum comparison, chained and nested
assignments, conditional branches and negated calls.

The Bedrock Molang reference documents string comparison and operator
precedence: https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-samples/Molang.html . Typed
wire-property decoding remains in the separate metadata feature.

Evaluate render-controller material arrays and bone overrides alongside actor inputs.
Retain ignore_lighting and the evaluated light_color_multiplier in immutable
selected models so a client renderer can preserve native draw state. These
properties are present in the saved protocol2193 Hive pack, including its
logo controller, and in the target Bedrock1.26.51.1 render-controller format.

Preserve the retained native actor scale from FLOAT metadata index 38 and
bind query.model_scale before controller evaluation. The protocol 2193
CubeCraft scene sends 1.7 for eleven banners and 5 for its hanging cube;
the target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 reference renders those larger meshes. Native
query.model_scale is registered at 1421f22c8 and its getter at 14221ac00
reads the float context member at +0x110. The documented query name is
model_scale, not scale: https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-samples/Molang.html .

Refresh custom models on scale-only changes so sparse metadata updates
reach both native client integrations and converted item displays. Multiply
the converter fit and origin translation by the actor scale, retaining
zero and negative values without introducing a Java living-entity clamp.
Client scripts.scale and camera-driven animations remain separate work.

Semantic tests decode the FLOAT wire value, retain it across unrelated
metadata updates, exercise equal-model refreshes, and select controllers
using query.model_scale.

Carry immutable controller UV offset/scale expression metadata in evaluated
models and include it in equality-based model updates. Protocol workers
preserve camera-dependent expressions for the native client's draw
context instead of evaluating them against unavailable camera state.
Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193) controller parser 141cabf60
initializes offset to zero and scale to one; ENTITY vertex DXIL applies
normalized UV times scale plus offset. The licensed executable SHA-256 is
537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7.
Model-selection tests evaluate retained lifetime expressions after a
snapshot replacement and check that UV-only changes refresh the model.
Native exports and saved server assets remain private.

Use the pinned MochaFloats 6.0.2 interpreter for single-precision values
and every arithmetic operation. Mocha 3.0.1 evaluates in doubles, so
casting only the returned value cannot reproduce native rounding in
controller conditions or assignments. Raise the core toolchain to Java
25 and migrate bindings to the fork's org.redlance namespace. Depend on
its interpreter modules without the unused bytecode compiler. Retain our
string comparison, assignment precedence and unary/postfix corrections.

The official Molang property guide documents float evaluation and the
16.7-million integer precision boundary. Shared regressions exercise
intermediate arithmetic, equality, assignments and property inputs:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/documents/introductiontoentityproperties

Fork source and build identity:
https://github.com/PlayerAnimationLibrary/mochafloats/tree/1a5dc0fc18bb7f8e7237c0de9cb66299362048ab

Retain named unused parameters in Checkstyle because independently
replayed upstream patches still compile on Java 17. Keep Java 25 syntax
and semantic checks active; the toolchain upgrade does not require a
mechanical rename across those unrelated patches.

Share native Molang easing and trigonometry in core with client animation.
Preserve directed rotation interpolation and the target's [-180,180)
angle reduction, including the float addition before the remainder.
The previous library sorted endpoints and repeatedly subtracted turns,
which reverses animations and can stall on large finite float values.

Target Bedrock 1.26.51.1/protocol 2193 constant dispatcher 140ae6140 and
rotation helper 140aeb870 match runtime operations 140b2c2b0 and
140b2d270 across 9,367 angle and interpolation cases. The private emulator
supplies numeric nodes and stacks, mocks eligibility and cleanup, and
supplies the fmodf import with host C float remainder. Native arithmetic
and runtime stack operations execute unchanged. This establishes these
math operations, not the complete parser or visible animation.
The official Molang reference describes directed shortest interpolation:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/reference/content/molangreference/examples/molangconcepts/mathfunctions/math_lerprotate
